WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.820
A public portfolio should show skill without exposing secrets, private data, or unsafe detail.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:17.480
Before publishing anything, assume that screenshots, logs, configuration files, diagrams, commit history, and sample data may contain more information than you intended.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.740
A careful privacy and safety review is part of the work, not an optional final polish step.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.140
Remove credentials and access-bearing values.

5
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:39.640
Do not publish real passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, signing keys, session secrets, connection strings, cloud access keys, or certificate private keys.

6
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.780
Do not include realistic-looking secrets for style.

7
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:51.360
Use obvious placeholders such as REDACTED_SECRET, EXAMPLE_API_KEY, FAKE_TOKEN_VALUE, or sample-user@example.invalid.

8
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.500
The goal is to teach or demonstrate safely, not to make examples look operational.

9
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.740
Remove private and confidential information.

10
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:15.440
That includes employer data, customer data, internal hostnames, private URLs, ticket numbers, employee names, email addresses, IP details when they reveal sensitive internal structure, proprietary architecture, and screenshots from private tools.

11
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:21.380
Even if a detail seems harmless by itself, combinations of details can reveal more than expected.

12
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.160
When in doubt, generalize or remove.

13
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.240
Be careful with offensive-security-adjacent work.

14
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:36.820
If you discuss a lab, capture-the-flag exercise, or intentionally vulnerable practice environment, keep the writeup clearly scoped and safe.

15
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.880
Do not encourage activity against real targets.

16
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.980
Do not publish step-by-step abuse paths that would enable misuse.

17
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:51.440
Focus on what you learned, how you documented scope, how you identified risk, and how a defender or developer would reduce that risk.

18
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.320
Review commit history, not just the latest files.

19
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.900
A secret removed from the current README may still appear in previous commits.

20
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.320
A screenshot replaced in the visible folder may still exist in history.

21
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:10.180
If sensitive data was committed, treat it seriously and remove or rotate the value as appropriate.

22
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.920
For portfolio projects, the safer pattern is to use fake values from the start.

23
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.920
A simple publication checklist helps.

24
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.100
Confirm that the project uses authorized or lab systems.

25
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.520
Confirm that all secrets are fake or redacted.

26
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.140
Confirm that screenshots do not reveal private information.

27
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.240
Confirm that sample data is safe.

28
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.260
Confirm that the README describes scope and limitations.

29
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.640
Confirm that the project does not imply experience or access you did not have.

30
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:47.420
Safe publishing shows ethics and judgment, which are core cybersecurity skills.
